                <pb facs="0356" n="244" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg244"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7043: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-06-14T18:00:00+08:00">June 14, 1935—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 14—1:38 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>276. Legation’s 274, June 14, noon. Following from American Consul General at
                    Mukden.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“June 14, 3 p.m. My June 13, noon. Local press today published obviously
                        inspired reports alleging that Chiang Kai-shek is supplying arms to Chahar
                        chairman and urging him to continue anti-Japanese activities and to maintain
                        contact with Outer Mongolia and Soviet Russia. Reports add that former
                        northern clique leaders are urging Chiang to endeavor embroil Japan lobby
                            [<hi rendition="#italic">military?</hi>] with powers having North China
                        interests.”</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
